  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Frontier models</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">GPT-6 Cyber</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">GPT-6 Cyber is OpenAI’s model for authorized security work, such as vulnerability research and exploit work, released 5 November 2026 according to <a href="https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-6-cyber">GPT-6 Cyber: Advancing the frontier of aligned cybersecurity agents</a> [1]. The <a href="https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-6-cyber/introduction">GPT-6 Cyber System Card</a> calls it “a cyber-specialized variant of <a href="/ai/models/openai/gpt-6-astra/">GPT-6 Astra</a>” [3]. It sits beside Astra. It does not replace it. Access is limited to approved Daybreak Red customers through the API and Codex [1]. List price is $20 input, $2 cached input, and $100 output per million tokens, per the <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6-cyber">GPT-6-Cyber model page</a> [2]. The API id is <code>gpt-6-cyber</code>.</p>
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />
    <ModelFacts />
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Training</h2>
    <p>OpenAI describes GPT-6 Cyber as a cyber-specialized variant of GPT-6 Astra designed for authorized security work [3]. Parameter count, training data, and knowledge cutoff are not in the sources on this page. Under its Preparedness Framework, OpenAI rates GPT-6 Cyber Critical in cybersecurity, up from High for GPT-5.6 Cyber [1]. The System Card gives it the same ratings as GPT-6 Astra: Critical in cybersecurity, High in biological and chemical, and below High in AI self-improvement [3].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Inference</h2>
    <p>The API endpoints are Responses and Chat Completions. Batch, Realtime, and fine-tuning are not supported [2]. The model page lists reasoning token support, and <code>gpt-6-cyber</code> is the only snapshot [2].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Context</h2>
    <p>The documented context window is 1,050,000 tokens. Max input is 922,000 tokens and max output is 128,000 tokens. Inputs are text and images. Output is text [2].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Methods</h2>
    <p>OpenAI says GPT-6 Cyber is available only to approved Daybreak Red users and organizations, with identity checks, single sign-on, multi-factor sign-in, and API IP allowlisting [3]. IP allowlisting is on by default for the API. Zero Data Retention customers generally need Private Safety Processing [1][3].</p>
    <p>On staying in scope, OpenAI says the model avoided out-of-scope exploitation in 99.93% of its ExploitGym Honeypot tests without the deployment prompt, up from 94.49% for GPT-5.6 Cyber, and in 100% with it [1]. The System Card shows the same results as 0.07% and 0% exploitation [3]. These are OpenAI’s own tests.</p>
    <p>OpenAI also reports field use [1]. It says its researchers reproduced 1,724 known Linux kernel vulnerabilities and built working exploits for 948 of them. GPT-5.6 Sol managed 166 on the same harness. These were known vulnerabilities, not 948 new bugs. OpenAI says it disclosed 304 critical Linux findings to maintainers. It also says the model found 81 previously unknown high-severity Firefox vulnerabilities, and that “Mozilla was supportive of this work, and began landing fixes shortly after receiving the reports.” OpenAI names Cisco, Calif, and CodeRabbit as users [1]. These are company claims, not outside checks.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Benchmarking</h2>
    <p>Every score in this section is OpenAI’s own, on cyber tests, several of which OpenAI built itself. None has been checked independently. OpenAI’s announce and its System Card use different comparison models for ExploitGym, so the table keeps them on separate rows.</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table ai-model-table--prose">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Eval</th>
            <th scope="col">GPT-6 Cyber</th>
            <th scope="col">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>ExploitGym (announce)</td><td>48.5%</td><td>Company claim [1]. GPT-5.6 Cyber: 32.1% on the same page.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ExploitGym (System Card)</td><td>48.45%</td><td>Company claim [3]. GPT-6 Astra: 42.43% on the same page.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ExploitGym Hardened</td><td>36.94%</td><td>Company claim [3]. GPT-6 Astra: 29.69%.</td></tr>
