    </div>
    <p>On RemoteExploitBench, a new OpenAI benchmark, OpenAI says GPT-6 Cyber completed 95% of tasks, while GPT-6 Astra running under Daybreak Blue refused all of them [1]. That gap mostly reflects access policy, since Astra refused, so it is not a measure of raw skill. OpenAI also says GPT-6 Cyber does not outperform GPT-6 Astra on general coding evaluations [3].</p>
    <p>OpenAI’s System Card reports results from Irregular, an outside evaluator [3]. Irregular found performance “broadly comparable to GPT-6 Astra’s, with higher success on long-horizon cyber tasks.” On FrontierCyber, GPT-6 Cyber solved 90 of 288 tasks against 96 of 288 for Astra. On CyScenarioBench it completed 11 of 11 scenarios at least once, against 8 of 11 for Astra [3]. These figures reach us through OpenAI’s document.</p>
    <p><a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/leaderboards/models">Artificial Analysis</a> has no Intelligence Index score for GPT-6 Cyber as of 6 November 2026 [4]. This card has no AA score, and the model has no row in the #aiBench table.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Access and pricing</h2>
    <p>OpenAI says GPT-6 Cyber is “initially available through the API and Codex to a select group of approved Daybreak Red customers,” and that it will expand access to all Daybreak Red customers soon [1]. OpenAI gives no date for that. An organization needs Daybreak Red access and approval to use it [2]. The sources on this page do not list it in ChatGPT.</p>
